g, T9 pung \u2014 also many pungs. So honors and terminals are being used.  \n\nT7 is a simple suit tile (number 7), not terminal. B3 is simple. Discarding B3 might be safer because B3 is in your exposed chow (B3,B4,B5), so opponents know you have B3, maybe they won't need it. But also, B3 is in the chow, so discarding B3 might not be expected.  \n\nT7 is unrelated to your melds. Discarding T7 might be safer because it's middle tile.  \n\nLooking at discard history: Previous player discarded B3 earlier (Play B3 in their history). Next player discarded B4, B5. B3 seems somewhat discarded already. T7: across player discarded T7, T8. So T7 also discarded.  \n\nI think discard B3 or T7 equally safe. But keeping T7 pair gives you a chance if you draw T7 and then B3. Keeping B3 gives you a chance if you draw B3 and then B3.  \n\nGiven B3 has 3 left, T7 has 3 left, equal probability. But with T7 pair, drawing T7 gives you a pung, which might score more (Pung of simples = 0 fans, but could help All Chows? No, you already have chows). Actually, your hand is all chows plus maybe a pung, which would break All Chows pattern. But scoring is not the primary concern now \u2014 winning is.  \n\nI'll choose to discard B3 to keep T7 pair, hoping to draw T7 soon and then B3.  \n\nReason: Keeping a pair is better for near-ready hands. Discarding B3 leaves you with T7 pair, waiting for T7 to make pung and then B3 to pair. Alternatively, if you draw B3 immediately after discarding B3, you'd have T7 pair and B3 single again \u2014 same as before. So no improvement. Actually, discarding B3 reduces your chances because you lose the B3 tile. Better to discard T7? Let's simulate:  \nOption 1: Discard B3 \u2192 hand: T7,T7 \u2192 need T7 + B3 pair (two specific tiles).  \nOption 2: Discard T7 \u2192 hand: T7,B3 \u2192 need T7 or B3 to make pair, then still need another meld? Actually with T7,B3, you have 2 tiles, 3 melds = 11 tiles, need 3 more tiles to form a meld and a pair (e.g., draw T7 makes T7 pair, then need two tiles for a meld). So that's 3 tiles away. Worse.  \n\nThus, discarding B3 is worse than discarding T7? Wait, discarding T7 leaves you with T7,B3 \u2192 that's two singles. You'd need to draw a pair of something and a meld, which is far.  \n\nDiscarding one T7 leaves you with T7,B3 \u2192 still two singles, same as above.  \n\nSo best is to discard B3, keeping T7 pair, because having a pair is closer to a winning condition (you need one more T7 to have a pung meld, then you just need a B3 pair). That's two specific tiles.  \n\nAlternatively, keeping B3 and discarding T7 leaves you with T7,B3 \u2192 two singles, needing to draw a pair and a meld \u2014 more tiles needed.  \n\nSo I'll discard B3.  \n\nFinal choice: Play B3.\n\nReason: Keeping the T7 pair provides a clearer path to completing a hand (by drawing T7 for a pung and then B3 for a pair), whereas discarding T7 leaves two unrelated singles requiring more draws. With limited tiles remaining, maintaining a pair is strategically better.\n\nAnswer: Play B3"}
